WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.060
OK, so let's get started
with today's lecture.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.680
So as you remember,
on Monday, we

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.720
looked at the optimal
control problem

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.200
through a closed-loop
lens whereby

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.200
the object we want to optimize
over is a closed loop policy.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.600
And we introduced the
most fundamental algorithm

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.120
that we have at our
disposal to compute

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.960
closed-loop optimal
policies, which is referred

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.800
to as dynamic programming.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.120
We derive the algorithm
for a discrete time

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.300
formulation of the problem.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.680
We will actually extend this
derivation to continuous time

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.640
next week, which will lead us
to the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.120
equation and the
reachability theory.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.280
And to do so, we discussed some
structure, some key structure

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.340
that the problem, the
optimal control problem as we

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.140
formulated [INAUDIBLE] that
we can leverage to derive

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.220
dynamic programming equations.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.780
And then I presented
the LQR as one example

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.900
that allowed me to
show you how to apply

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.060
the dynamic
programming, recursion.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.300
And in time, LQR is
a problem that is

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.620
of interest in its own right.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.220
Now in the second
pset, you have a number

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.580
of opportunities to
strengthen your familiarity

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.140
with dynamic
programming and LQR.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.900
You have one exercise that
shows how much computation

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.940
you save by running dynamic
programming as a solution

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.500
algorithm as opposed
to brute force.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.040
You have one exercise that
forces you to implement

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.620
from scratch an LQR algorithm.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.260
I know that you
could use an AI agent

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.700
to do so, but at least
for once in your life,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.388
try to do it from scratch.

00:01:55.388 --> 00:01:57.180
And then we'll transition
into the material

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.000
that we're going
to discuss today.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Specifically, we
talked about LQR

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.800
at the end of the last lecture.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.680
Today, rather than introducing a
new concept in optimal control,

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.840
we are actually going to
spend an entire lecture

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.400
on how we can leverage LQR in
a variety of different control

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:17.900
settings.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.680
As I said, LQR is
a formulation that

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.120
is of interest in
its own right, what

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.280
we use in the field of controls
and robotics and beyond.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.320
So I'm going to dig a little bit
deeper on how you can use LQR

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.840
as part of the control loop.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.480
Then next week, we'll
generalize the formulation

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.940
to stochastic formulation.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.760
So we'll assume the
dynamics are perturbed

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.280
by stochastic disturbances.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.440
That will lead us to
the stochastic version

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.760
of dynamic programming
equations that you

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.700
may be already familiar with if
you have taken a class Decision

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.480
Making Under Uncertainty.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.700
And then next Wednesday, we
will cover the continuous time

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.760
formulation of closed-loop
optimal control,

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.060
which will lead us to the
extension of the dynamic

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.120
programming algorithms
in continuous time,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.260
referred to as the
Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.580
equation.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.820
After that, we'll
then move to MPC, just

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.780
to give you an idea
about where we're going.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.700
So as I said, today, we're going
to focus on LQR-style algorithms

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.080
for optimal control.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.440
I will claim that
after PID control,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.060
LQR is the most widely
controlled theoretical algorithm

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.260
used out there.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.340
So it's very important
for you to familiarize

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:40.220
with the concept, particularly
LQR, as we set it up.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.300
We set it up with the goal
of finding a control that

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.700
drives the state to the origin.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.060
That's why it's referred to
as linear-quadratic regulator.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.540
The goal is to do regulation to
drive the state to the origin.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.000
But in general, especially
in a robotic system,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.160
you're not that interested in
driving the state to the origin.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.600
You want to drive the state
toward a desired behavior

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.920
or a desired trajectory.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.600
Can we still do that with LQR?

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.220
And the answer is yes.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.400
And we look at how to use LQR
in this setting by looking

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.459
at LQR as a mechanism to
do trajectory tracking,

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.780
both in a linear setting
and a nonlinear setting.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:23.280
Yeah.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:23.780
Hi.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.600
You said that this is the
most common outside of PID.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.640
I was wondering if you could
talk maybe for 30 seconds

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.080
or so of why you would
choose this over PID

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.680
instead of [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.680
Well, the problem with PID
is that you don't really

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.800
have a notion of an
objective function.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.920
In LQR, you can
more intentionally

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.120
specify your objectives.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.120
There are indirect ways to shape
the response of your system

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.820
with PID controllers.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.500
And if you have taken a class
like ENGINEERING 105 or 205,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.580
you spend quite a
bit of time in terms

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.780
of mapping relationships
between phase margin

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.740
and gain margin or placement
of poles with the rise time

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:05.360
and so on.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.020
But it's a bit indirect.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:13.260
With the LQR, you have
a very explicit handle

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.900
on how to optimize operations.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.740
But on top of it, LQR,
as we will see today,

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:19.920
is very flexible.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.840
It can be used not just
for stabilization purposes,

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.620
but also for trajectory
tracking purposes,

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:33.580
and even as a tool to compute
trajectories for optimal control

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.060
problems.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:41.023
Would you say that [INAUDIBLE]
is simple to implement?

00:05:41.023 --> 00:05:42.440
Actually, it's
very complementary.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:48.260
So typically, PID is used as
the last step of your control

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:50.820
hierarchy when you really have
to manipulate the signals that

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.760
go into the actuators
literally throttle, steering,

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.000
opening valves, and such.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.040
LQR is set just above PIDs.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.560
This one's more
about trajectories.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.520
So don't view them as in
opposition to each other.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.120
They're very much complementary.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.720
And by the end of
this class, we'll

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.680
have a bit more of a
discussion about how you

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.840
bring all these tools together.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.720
Typically, the way that
a decision-making logic

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.440
is architected is that
you have typically

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:27.400
PID at the end of the
day at the lowest level.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.520
Then you have some
trajectory optimizer

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.180
which could be LQR-derived.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.640
It could be SCP.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.760
It could be some
other direct method.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.220
On top of it, you still
have to set objectives.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:38.520
So how do you do that?

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.160
You can use techniques
from decision-making.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.160
These days, maybe
you may want to use

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.780
more data-driven techniques.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.000
You can use large language
models, vision language models.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.220
And the difference
between these layers

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.020
is essentially the
granularity at which

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.740
the physical knowledge
and physical properties

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.953
of the system are leveraged.

00:06:59.953 --> 00:07:01.620
Typically, the level
of decision-making,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:05.460
you don't really reason
about all the nuanced aspects

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.520
of your dynamics.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.260
For example, in
your brain, if you

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.380
want to set the goal
of moving to that door,

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.100
you don't really reason about
all the joint displacement

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.620
in your arms and so on.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.800
When you start optimizing
for your trajectory,

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.860
probably you take into account
that you are a dynamical system.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.880
You need to have some
space around you.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.430
But still, you're not thinking
about all the joint level.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.180
And then you start
going down [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.460
Does that make sense?

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.760
Yeah, very good question.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:32.280
All right.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.420
So as I said, we're going to
look at LQR as an algorithm

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:42.580
to derive tracking
algorithms, also as a way

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:45.900
to devise a new way to compute
trajectories for optimal control

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.180
problems, which
sounds a bit weird.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.920
So we derive LQR as a
solution for optimal control

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.760
problem in a very specific
setting of linear dynamics

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:54.860
and quadratic cost.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.200
We'll see how that then
can be used as a way

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:01.600
to derive a new direct
methods for optimal control.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.840
It's a bit confusing,
but we'll get there.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.800
And then we'll look
at this algorithm

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.340
referred to as iterative LQR.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:08.220
Quite popular.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.678
And then we're going to look
at the refinement of iterative

00:08:10.678 --> 00:08:12.980
LQR referred to as differential
dynamic programming.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:16.000
All of this is
rooted in LQR ideas.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.800
Now some of the equations will
get a little bit convoluted.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.760
For algebraic purposes, there is
nothing particularly complicated

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:25.480
here.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.200
So I won't be printing
all of the update

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.120
equations for the different
matrices and gains and so on.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.640
Everything is explicitly
provided in the lecture notes

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.740
that are referenced
in this slide,

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.047
and are also referenced
in the syllabus.

00:08:42.047 --> 00:08:43.380
All right, so let's get started.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.440
So first of all,
a recap about LQR.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:51.700
LQR is a very specific class of
an optimal control problem where

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.920
we don't have
constraints on the state,

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.140
we don't have constraints
on the control.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.300
The dynamics are linear.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.940
xk equal A xk plus Bk uk.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.520
Note that the dynamics are not
necessarily time-invariant.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:11.540
Actually, the state matrix
A and B can depend on time,

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.060
as denoted by the subscript k.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:17.140
This will become relevant
later, why actually this

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.340
is a useful property of LQR.

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:23.060
And then we assume that
we have a quadratic cost.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:28.960
So last time, we looked
at the cost until here.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:32.300
So we have a quadratic
penalty on the terminal state

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:36.460
plus a running cost that entails
a quadratic penalty on the state

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:38.820
and quadratic penalty
on the control.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.160
You can slightly
generalize this cost.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.880
And that changes nothing in
terms of derivations by also

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:50.320
including cross
terms, x times u.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.040
Why would you like to include
this cross term into the cost

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:54.800
function?

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.000
Well, maybe in some
situations, the amount

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.640
to which you want to
penalize the control effort

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.800
might depend on the
state you are in.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.560
But more mechanically,
when we'll

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.120
be using a LQR as a
mechanism to devise

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.560
tracking control
algorithms, that

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.660
will entail performing
some linearization.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:18.160
So this additional
cross terms will pop up

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.380
in the cost function.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:22.400
So that's why I want to present
the equation this slightly more

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.360
generalized form.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.800
You can write the cost function
here a bit more compactly

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.160
in matrix form.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.800
You have the quadratic
term on the state

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:38.000
x transpose Q x quadratic on
the controls u transpose R u.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.800
And then you have
the cross terms

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:45.100
x H u, u H x that sum
together and give you

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.900
the equation at the top.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:48.880
So the Bellman
equation, as usual,

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.460
entails minimizing
over the control

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:56.560
at time k, the current stage
cost, plus the cost-to-go.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.820
And we know that in a QR, the
cost-to-go is also quadratic,

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:05.580
specifically if you do the
derivation same as last time.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:12.900
So you start from the boundary
condition, which is a quadratic,

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.500
because of how we set
up the terminal cost.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:19.820
And then you go one step later,
you set the gradient equal to 0.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.220
Then you solve for the control.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.120
And then you plug that back
into the right-hand side

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:28.120
of the Bellman equation.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.940
You see that the cost
is also quadratic.

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:32.960
This stays exactly the same.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:36.240
So the addition of the cross
term doesn't change anything.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.060
So you will find that the
optimal control law is still

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:42.480
a linear feedback in control.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.640
So it's a matrix times state.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:50.000
And the optimal cost is
a quadratic in the state.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.280
Of course, L and
P update equations

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.120
will change slightly because
now we have a cross term.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.580
Details of which are
in the lecture notes.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:02.920
Not too important here to
show the exact equations.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.680
You can generalize
this formulation

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:14.320
even further by now introducing
not only cross terms,

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.960
but also linear terms in
the state and in the control

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.200
and also constant terms.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.320
So now you have a cost function
that is quadratic in the state,

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.360
quadratic in a control,
linear in the state,

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:30.600
linear in control,
plus a constant term.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.800
The main reason why we are
interested in this kind

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:35.080
of generalization
is because when

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.820
we are going to use LQR for
tracking purposes [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:40.320
perform linearizations, you
have these additional terms that

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:41.780
pop up.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:44.760
And you can even for LQR
generalize the dynamics.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:50.700
So looking at the
setting whereby xk plus 1

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:59.860
is equal to Ak xk
plus Bk Uk plus dk.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:01.660
So now the dynamics
are no longer linear.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.700
They're referred to
as affine because you

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.580
have a constant forcing term.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.908
Think of this term
perhaps a disturbance,

00:13:07.908 --> 00:13:09.700
like, for example, a
wind gust that we know

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.820
will happen at time k.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:14.860
Well, you can do exactly
the same derivation

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:16.480
we did for standard LQR.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:18.800
So we write down the
Bellman equation.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:21.980
We start from the
final condition,

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:25.620
which is now a quadratic plus
linear plus constant terms.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.640
We set the gradient equal to 0.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:29.100
We solve for u.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.380
Then we plug that back
into the right-hand side

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:32.580
of the Bellman equation.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:36.340
So what you will find is
that the optimal control

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:42.920
policy is a linear feedback
term plus a feedforward term.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:46.400
And the cost function
is a quadratic

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:50.840
that also has linear
terms and constant terms.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:51.600
Yeah.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.160
So the equation
when you're using

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:54.800
for recapping that
we're minimizing

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:56.967
was slightly different than
the one you talked about

00:13:56.967 --> 00:13:57.900
in last lecture.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:13:58.400
Yeah.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:02.100
So here, the main difference is
that I'm adding this cross term.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.640
And my main point is that
when you do the derivation,

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:07.840
the structure of the control
law and the optimal cost

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:08.700
does not change.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.800
You still have linear
feeding from the state

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.020
and quadratic cost.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.360
Of course, the gain and the P
are going to change slightly.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:19.000
But also, instead of
using QN, you already

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:21.860
had the H was there instead.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.960
It was switched like your
terminal state was using

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.520
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:29.080
[INAUDIBLE] terminology?

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:32.240
So you're saying
that here I had an H.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.040
H. And then there
was no H, though,

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:35.140
when you were summing it.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.420
So it made it seem
like it was completely

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.980
a different matrix that
you're multiplying against.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:41.540
Does that--

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:43.720
So here, instead
of QN, we had what?

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:44.220
The H.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:45.600
OK, that's fine.

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.220
That's just a terminology.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:47.720
Yeah, OK.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:49.030
But then there wasn't any--

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:51.280
for example, there's a Q in
there, but there is also--

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:51.780
[INAUDIBLE]

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:52.940
[INAUDIBLE]

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:56.300
Yeah, 100% So what I'm saying
is that what we had last time

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.500
was everything up to here.

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.540
Yeah.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:04.840
But this Q is from-- it's also
like that matrix [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:08.940
[INAUDIBLE] It's
still just any--

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:09.523
Oh, it's not--

00:15:09.523 --> 00:15:10.982
[INAUDIBLE] targets
that you choose

00:15:10.982 --> 00:15:11.680
OK, it's not--

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:16.100
I'm going to call
it like [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.380
I couldn't [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:17.880
OK.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.422
So don't think of
it as the same key.

00:15:19.422 --> 00:15:20.440
No, no, no, no.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.920
This is just a generic matrix
that you choose as a designer.

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:28.740
So the idea of LQR is that
once there's a type of problem,

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:29.960
then everything is procedure.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.752
You automatically get
to the optimal policy

00:15:31.752 --> 00:15:32.900
and the optimal cost.

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:35.220
Now the bargain on
you is not to solve

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:37.520
the problem is to set it up.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:43.880
So your bargain is to pick
R, to pick Q, to pick H,

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:45.720
and to pick QR.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.120
And this is not always obvious.

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:51.160
So this is called LQR tuning.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:57.880
It takes quite a bit actually
to pick these parameters

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:01.920
in such a way that you
get your desired response.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:07.040
Like, for example, when I was
tuning the LQR base, actually

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.680
tracking algorithm
for the [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.800
it took about two
or three months

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:16.680
to actually get exactly the
parameters that you really

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.000
want so that you shape
the right lateral response

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.080
and longitudinal response.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.100
You want it to be,
of course, safe,

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:26.620
but also, you want it to be
very pleasant for the user.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:29.847
So there is a little bit of
an art and a little bit--

00:16:29.847 --> 00:16:31.680
of course, you can use
simulation to do that

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:32.638
and so on and so forth.

00:16:32.638 --> 00:16:34.440
But the point being,
those matrices

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:38.900
are just placeholder
symbols that you should then

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:41.980
select as a designer.

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:45.560
Even further, as I said, here,
we are adding additional terms.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:46.900
So we have the ck.

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.860
That is something that you
will pick as a designer.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:51.440
We have these vectors 2k.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:53.980
This is something that you
will pick as a designer,

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:55.980
or as you will see
when we do tracking.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.780
They are derived from a
linearization process.

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:02.380
But the main point here is that
in this more general setup,

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:04.560
the idea, the derivation
is exactly the same.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:09.140
Actually, you may want to
try to do it by yourself.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:13.140
And what you will get is that
the optimal control policy now

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:14.400
is a little bit different.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:21.900
So it still entail linear
feedback on the state.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.099
For example, to your
point, this thing

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:27.900
here in the previous
lecture was called fk.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.260
So, yeah, sorry for the mix-up.

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:35.680
Lk xk plus Lk.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:38.140
So we have a linear
feedback on the state.

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:41.200
This is also what we had in
the most basic version of LQR.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:43.820
But now we have a
feedforward term.

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:47.240
So a constant gain.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:50.000
Actually, there is a very
intuitive interpretation

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.900
of this control law structure.

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:55.320
I'll come back to that later.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.480
And likewise, now
the optimal cost

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:02.240
is no longer just
the x transpose P x.

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:06.680
Also has linear terms in
x and then a constant.

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:13.580
All these values Pk, matrix
Pk, vector pk, constant pk, Lk,

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:16.160
capital K, and so
on are all derived

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:19.180
through Riccati-like
equations, update equations.

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:20.680
The specific
implementation of which

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:23.263
is provided in the notes that
are a little bit cumbersome, not

00:18:23.263 --> 00:18:25.960
super useful to show here.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:29.760
So bottom line is that LQR
can be generalized somewhat

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.580
to cost, where you also have
linear terms, constant terms,

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:37.022
and cross penalty terms, and
to dynamics that are affine.

00:18:37.022 --> 00:18:38.980
And the structure of the
optimal control policy

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:41.700
stays either the
same or very similar,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:44.220
modulo the addition
of a feedforward term,

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.320
and also the structure
of the optimal cost

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.860
say essentially the
same, modulo the addition

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:51.740
of some linear terms
and constant terms.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:52.700
That's it.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:56.060
Let's get to the
interesting part here.

00:18:56.060 --> 00:19:03.540
So as I said last time, we
looked at LQR as a mechanism

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:06.100
to find controls that
allow us to regulate

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:08.780
the state of the origin.

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:15.020
But in general, we
are interested in more

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:20.180
general settings whereby we are
given some reference trajectory

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:33.840
x bar 0, x bar 1,
x bar 2, and so on.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:38.420
So we have the reference
trajectory x bar 0, x bar 1,

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:40.810
x bar n minus 1--

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:42.140
actually xm.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:53.600
And then u bar 0, u
bar 1, u bar n minus 1.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.920
Of course, the control
trajectory and the state

00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:58.480
trajectory are not independent
of each other, are linked

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.160
through the system equations.

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:03.800
X is derived through
the application of u

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:06.000
through the system equations.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:09.240
Where is this reference
trajectory coming from?

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:12.120
Maybe from one of the
[INAUDIBLE] methods

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:13.100
we covered last time.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:16.520
So, for example, you run SCP
on your general nonlinear

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:18.200
optimal control problem.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:24.720
You computed a trajectory
in open-loop form.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:28.320
And that's the trajectory
that you would like to follow.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:31.740
But now the problem
is that in real life,

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:33.380
you have model mismatches.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:35.260
You have disturbances.

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:41.480
So maybe you may not
start exactly at x bar 0.

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:42.740
Maybe you start here.

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:46.060
Maybe there is some disturbance
that brings you here.

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:53.660
So the question is, how do
we find a control u that

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:58.580
brings the actual trajectory as
close as possible to the desired

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:00.620
pre-computed trajectory?

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:03.740
That's the problem of
trajectory tracking.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:11.300
Specifically, we want to
find a sequence of controls.

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:13.500
I put empty bracket
here to mean that we

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:17.660
want to find the full sequence
of controls u0, u1, u2,

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:19.300
all the way to u n minus 1.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:24.440
Somewhat close.

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:26.800
And that will be
more specific later.

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:36.055
Somewhat close to the
nominal control sequence,

00:21:36.055 --> 00:21:38.180
because that's the one that
we would like to track.

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:40.597
We want to track both the state
trajectory and the control

00:21:40.597 --> 00:21:50.000
trajectory such that the
tracking error defined

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:53.220
as the difference between
the realized trajectory,

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:58.160
the sequence of states that your
system actually goes through,

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:01.460
and the desired trajectory--

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:05.640
so the sequence of states you
would have liked to go through,

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.080
as specified by the
pre-computed trajectory--

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:11.860
is minimized.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:19.680
Does this sound like
a reasonable problem?

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:21.260
You're given a
nominal trajectory.

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:26.260
You want to find a sequence
of controls that bring you

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:30.640
back to that trajectory.

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:31.480
So you want to--

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:34.320
so this will be delta x1.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.220
For some reason, some
disturbance moved you here.

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.860
Some disturbance that was
unmodeled when you computed

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:44.260
your nominal trajectory.

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:46.800
Now you want to get back
to your nominal trajectory.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:53.160
So what can you do?

00:22:56.060 --> 00:23:00.620
Let's first look at the
simplest possible case.

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:04.540
Not useful, but it allows to
scaffold intuition, whereby

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:07.360
the underlying
dynamics are linear.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:14.803
So xk plus 1 is equal
to Ak xk plus Bk uk.

00:23:14.803 --> 00:23:16.220
So for now, we
assume the dynamics

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.780
are linear time-varying.

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:23.900
Now, the reference trajectory
or nominal trajectory

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:25.480
or desired trajectory--

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:28.360
these are all
interchangeable terms--

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:32.600
it has to be a feasible
trajectory for your system.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:34.880
So your nominal
trajectory also has

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:45.920
to satisfy x bar k plus 1
equal Ak x bar k plus Bk uk.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:52.240
Again, a reference trajectory
is a feasible trajectory

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:53.220
for your system.

00:23:56.960 --> 00:23:57.980
For system.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:08.360
So then let's just take
instead of reasoning

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:13.000
in terms of absolute states or
absolute controls, let's reason

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.580
in terms of deviation variables.

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:17.360
So we take the difference
between these two equations.

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:21.160
What we get is that
delta xk plus 1

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:29.740
equal Ak delta xk plus
Bk delta uk, where

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:32.900
delta uk are the division
variables with respect

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:33.800
to the control.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:37.800
So they are defined
as uk minus u bar k.

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:45.060
So then you can see how the
problem of tracking actually

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:48.593
has been reformulated
as an LQR problem

00:24:48.593 --> 00:24:50.260
with respect to the
deviation variables.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:51.100
Why?

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:56.420
Because our objective is
to bring delta x to 0.

00:24:56.420 --> 00:24:58.180
Now we have a system
that is purely

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:02.500
defined in terms of delta
x's and deviation variables.

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:04.160
And we want to
bring delta x to 0.

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:05.700
So now we can see
that the problem

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:09.180
is to choose the deviation
variables delta u

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.760
such that delta x's goes to 0.

00:25:11.760 --> 00:25:15.100
If that happens, it means
that xk goes to x bar k.

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:18.760
And so you have
satisfied your objective.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:30.940
Of course, when you run
your system, you do not--

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:33.400
and this sometimes is
a point of confusion.

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:35.280
You do not control
your system directly

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:36.620
with your deviation variables.

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:37.920
You have to go back.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:41.200
The control that actually you
will inject in your system

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:48.760
is equal to uk bar plus
delta uk because you

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:52.560
know that once you use
uk as your control,

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:55.920
then you go back into
the difference equation.

00:25:55.920 --> 00:26:00.040
You will have the different--
the system in terms of deviation

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:03.160
variables is guaranteed
to converge to 0,

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:05.940
if you pick delta u such
that delta x goes to 0.

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:09.920
But how do you pick delta u
such that delta x goes to 0?

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:12.800
You set up an
auxiliary LQR problem

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:18.280
whereby you set up
a cost function that

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:21.860
is quadratic in the deviation
variables for the state

00:26:21.860 --> 00:26:24.980
and quadratic in the deviation
variables for the controls.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.560
You pick R, Q and QN.

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:29.660
These are the design parameters.

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:33.780
And then you just solve
LQR with a Riccati equation

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:45.280
that will give you the optimal
delta uk as Lk times delta xk.

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:51.180
And then you have found your
desired sequence of controls

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:53.700
to track your trajectory.

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:56.300
Does this make sense?

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:58.260
So we started from
a tracking problem

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:01.100
that a face value
didn't have much--

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:05.940
didn't look quite different
from a regulation problem.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.760
But then by reasoning in
terms of deviation variables,

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:11.140
we were able to reformulate
the tracking problem

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:14.020
as a regulation problem with
respect to deviation variables.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:16.820
We then do control
development in the space

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:18.720
of deviation variables.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:23.600
And to do that, we formulate an
auxiliary LQR problem with cost

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:26.240
also defined [INAUDIBLE]
deviation variables.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:30.160
And once you have found the
optimal control law with respect

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:32.480
to the deviation
variables, you go back

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:34.320
to the original
control variables

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:36.080
that are the ones
that you actually

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.860
inject into your system.

00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:42.240
And then you guarantee that
with this choice, the deviation

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:45.120
variables delta x
will go to 0 simply

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:50.480
by plugging the definition
of delta u into the equation.

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:51.420
Does that make sense?

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:02.780
So look at now the
structure of your control.

00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:21.660
So now the structure of
your tracking controller

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:26.740
has a feedforward term that
is your nominal control.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:31.060
Of course, if your state is
exactly equal to your desired

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:33.700
state, this term is 0.

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:36.920
This means that you are
on the nominal trajectory,

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:39.138
so you should indeed
use the nominal control.

00:28:39.138 --> 00:28:40.180
So that's a sanity check.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:41.953
That makes sense.

00:28:41.953 --> 00:28:43.120
We'll look at the structure.

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:44.620
So we have a
feedforward term, which

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:50.660
is your nominal control
plus a feedback term

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:54.580
that depending on how
far the actual state is

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:57.580
from the reference
state, it pushes you back

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:01.420
into your reference trajectory
mediated through this k.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:05.620
So it's an open-loop term
plus a closed-loop term.

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:07.980
So this structure is
actually widely used

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:09.440
in controls and robotics.

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:18.320
It's referred to as a
two-step design, whereby

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:20.120
the idea is that you
first use techniques

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:23.160
from open-loop control to
find your nominal trajectory.

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:26.720
And then you use techniques
for closed-loop control

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:31.160
to find a controller that
locally tries to push you back

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:35.520
onto your desired trajectory.

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:36.420
Why is this useful?

00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:41.300
It tries to balance the two
competing considerations.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.960
On the one hand, you want
the computational efficiency

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:47.023
of reason in terms of
open-loop trajectories.

00:29:47.023 --> 00:29:49.440
But at the same time, you want
to have the robustness that

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:51.280
comes from closed-loop control.

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:53.560
And here how everything
is coming together.

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:55.160
You use the techniques
from last week

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:58.080
to compute this object,
and then the techniques

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.820
from this week to compute
a closed-loop controller.

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:04.400
Of course, you could solve
the optimal control problem

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:06.920
in closed loop
globally, but that

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:09.640
would be extremely
time-consuming and perhaps

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:11.360
not even useful.

00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:12.340
Why is not even useful?

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:15.340
And then I will get
to your questions.

00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:16.780
Because right now
the formulation

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:19.860
that we have looked
at is static.

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:25.180
So the constraints
are given and so on.

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:28.180
But for a robotics application,
for example, the environment

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:29.240
is dynamic.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:32.480
So I have now the current
situation of this room.

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:33.740
I plan my path.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:36.780
But maybe one of your
colleagues stands up.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:38.420
And now my constraint
set changes

00:30:38.420 --> 00:30:41.540
because I also have a new
additional collision constraints

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:47.620
that I don't want to bump
into the CLI if she stands up,

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:51.070
which means that in general you
have to reformulate this problem

00:30:51.070 --> 00:30:52.820
and recompute the
solution to this problem

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:54.220
periodically in time.

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:56.460
So solving for a
closed-loop control

00:30:56.460 --> 00:31:00.100
law that applies globally for
all possible states of the world

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:01.833
may even be wasteful
because in case you

00:31:01.833 --> 00:31:04.900
know that you will
never reach a state that

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:06.500
is very far enough
from where you

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:10.740
are before you have to
recompute anyway your problem.

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:12.200
Does that make sense?

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:12.700
All right.

00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:14.160
So question?

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:17.420
I was going to ask, is
the gain term L there?

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:21.580
Is that positive, or does that
also change as you [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:22.340
No.

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:26.880
This is a-- well, it's constant,
but the subscript k here

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:29.560
means that it
depends on the stage.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:31.280
So it's time-varying.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:36.600
Of course, if you change the
formulation of the LQR problem,

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:37.700
Lk would change.

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.720
But otherwise, no, Lk
does not change or is not

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:42.320
a function of the state.

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:46.680
It's a purely constant gain
matrix given the current stage.

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:47.880
Yeah.

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:49.880
Just a clarifying question.

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:56.000
So we get the bars from-- let's
say we did [INAUDIBLE] and then

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:57.760
reformulated by getting
the delta where--

00:31:57.760 --> 00:31:59.760
I guess I'm a little bit
confused where when you

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:01.400
got the delta of the uk.

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:04.600
Now you know uk, and
you know the bars--

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:08.160
No, you don't know uk.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:10.300
This is just the definition
of change of variable.

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:11.475
This is you know it.

00:32:11.475 --> 00:32:11.975
You know it.

00:32:11.975 --> 00:32:14.064
OK.

00:32:14.064 --> 00:32:15.540
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.030
This is you do not know it.

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:17.780
You don't know it.

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:19.820
And then solve for--
you just solve for--

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:21.780
You just solve for
delta u, though.

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:23.900
And once you actually
reason about delta u

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:26.660
and you assign a value to
delta u, then you can--

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:29.100
And that's a time-varying.

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:29.900
Definitely.

00:32:29.900 --> 00:32:31.140
That's right.

00:32:31.140 --> 00:32:32.980
Yeah.

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:35.000
So this seems reasonable.

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:36.420
On the other hand,
all of this was

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:40.740
derived under the assumption
of linear dynamics.

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.620
A more interesting
setting is the setting

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:45.820
where you have a nonlinear
dynamical system.

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:52.660
So, how do you do tracking when
now the dynamics are nonlinear?

00:32:52.660 --> 00:32:57.180
It turns out that we use
essentially the same techniques

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:00.980
we have just discussed.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:04.380
So specifically, let's assume
that now our system dynamics

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:05.160
are nonlinear.

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:08.880
So xk plus 1 is
equal to f of xk uk.

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:11.600
Let's assume as before
we're given a reference

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:15.900
trajectory again computed
through a direct method,

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:17.800
for example through SCP.

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:21.600
Now the question again is to
find a sequence of controls u

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:24.160
that bring your state
trajectory and also

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:26.680
control trajectory
as close as possible

00:33:26.680 --> 00:33:30.080
to their nominal counterparts.

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:33.000
On the other hand,
though, now the dynamics

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:36.800
are no longer linear.

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:50.720
So we have that your
system dynamics of A xk

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:54.280
plus 1 equal of xk uK.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:57.600
And then you have that--
your nominal trajectory which

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:00.880
has to be a feasible
trajectory satisfies xk plus 1

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:07.020
equal f of xk uk delta.

00:34:10.780 --> 00:34:13.100
Can we still reformulate
the tracking problem

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:15.260
in the deviation variables?

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:20.340
Yes, if we apply
the following trick.

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:33.340
So xk plus 1 minus
x bar k plus 1

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:49.060
is equal to f of x uk minus
f of x bar k, u bar k.

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:54.860
But now let's do
the usual trick.

00:34:54.860 --> 00:34:57.700
So let's do a Taylor
expansion, whereby

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:01.700
we approximate f
around its argument

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:06.480
x and u evaluated
at x bar and u bar.

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:09.840
So if you do so,
then we can write

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:14.920
the f of xu as f
of x bar k comma

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:20.360
u bar k plus gradient
of f with respect

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:29.280
to x times xk minus x bar k plus
gradient of f with respect to u.

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:34.720
Multiply the uk minus u bar k.

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:35.460
Jacobians.

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:35.960
Yeah.

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:46.040
So let's call these
derivatives as Ak.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:52.720
Let's call these
derivatives as Bk.

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:57.320
So once we plug this
formulation in here,

00:35:57.320 --> 00:36:15.380
what we get is xk
plus 1 minus x bar k.

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:21.380
So I'm just substituting in here
the linear approximation of fx

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:22.153
in view.

00:36:22.153 --> 00:36:22.820
So what we get--

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:34.380
this is equal to f x bar k comma
u bar k plus Ak xk minus x bar k

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:46.300
plus Bk uk minus u bar k
minus f of x bar k, u bar k.

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:48.260
So these two terms cancel out.

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:51.940
We define as before
the deviation variable

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:56.980
as xk minus x bar k.

00:36:56.980 --> 00:37:01.060
So this is the deviation
variable for x of k plus 1.

00:37:01.060 --> 00:37:03.990
This is the deviation
variable for u.

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:06.570
This is the deviation
variable for x.

00:37:09.950 --> 00:37:12.790
[INAUDIBLE]

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:14.550
When you sorry?

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:20.790
So when you first [INAUDIBLE]
somewhat true if [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:21.530
That's right.

00:37:21.530 --> 00:37:24.830
But they're not [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:25.910
No.

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:26.590
Yes.

00:37:26.590 --> 00:37:29.090
Well, so this is a
tracking problem.

00:37:29.090 --> 00:37:33.590
So typically, you start very
close to the initial condition

00:37:33.590 --> 00:37:36.690
that you used in order to
get your nominal trajectory.

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:38.190
And the idea of a
tracking algorithm

00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:41.110
is actually to stay
close to your tracking--

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:43.190
to your nominal trajectory.

00:37:43.190 --> 00:37:47.150
So in that sense,
the problem is such

00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:50.390
that you want to stay close to
that nominal trajectory, which

00:37:50.390 --> 00:37:52.670
means that the
linearization typically

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:55.010
is a good approximation
because you're close enough.

00:37:55.010 --> 00:37:55.650
But it's true.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:58.510
There is always the danger that
you are deviating a bit too

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:02.330
much, in which case the
linearization becomes useless.

00:38:02.330 --> 00:38:06.250
And this algorithm
could become erratic.

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:09.290
In practice, it
works quite well.

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:12.570
But yeah, it's a
very valid point.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:19.770
But the main point here is that
as you saw, if we just by this

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:23.650
approximation that the
dynamics can be linearized,

00:38:23.650 --> 00:38:26.450
we are back to
the linear setting

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:31.050
whereby the dynamics for our
deviation variables are linear,

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:34.170
which means that again, I
can set up an auxiliary LQR

00:38:34.170 --> 00:38:39.850
problem with respect to the
delta u and delta x variables,

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:43.690
find the optimal delta
u, and then backtrack

00:38:43.690 --> 00:38:47.850
the tracking algorithm-- the
tracking control signal as u bar

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:52.370
k plus delta uk.

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:54.450
So once basically
you do linearization,

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:57.450
you're back again
into the setup we

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:01.470
had in the previous
slide or linear dynamics.

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:03.190
Something that I
want to note here

00:39:03.190 --> 00:39:13.950
is that these Jacobians here
are evaluated at xk uk, xk uk.

00:39:13.950 --> 00:39:18.930
You are linearizing essentially
around the nominal state.

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:24.190
You should be at time k,
time 1 in this example, which

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:26.590
essentially means that
these Jacobians are actually

00:39:26.590 --> 00:39:28.110
time-dependent.

00:39:28.110 --> 00:39:29.790
The dependency
stems from the fact

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:32.390
that you are linearizing at
different stages with respect

00:39:32.390 --> 00:39:34.870
to different points
on the trajectory.

00:39:34.870 --> 00:39:42.030
Fortunately, LQR works for
linear time-varying dynamics.

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:45.601
So this is not a problem here.

00:39:45.601 --> 00:39:49.080
[INAUDIBLE]

00:39:52.559 --> 00:39:53.310
Yeah, yeah.

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:53.810
Absolutely.

00:39:53.810 --> 00:39:55.060
If you had a feasible trajec--

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.570
but in reality, what
happens is that you are,

00:39:57.570 --> 00:39:59.290
again, a robotic system.

00:39:59.290 --> 00:40:01.890
You're moving around
in your environment.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:04.330
And new stuff happens,
so you compute

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:05.830
a new open-loop trajectory.

00:40:05.830 --> 00:40:09.530
So that's why in some settings,
you cannot really pre-compute

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:11.750
the linearized Jacobians.

00:40:11.750 --> 00:40:14.810
But if actually the environment
is static, then absolutely,

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:15.590
you can do that.

00:40:15.590 --> 00:40:16.890
You can do that.

00:40:16.890 --> 00:40:20.173
[INAUDIBLE]

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:23.390
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:40:23.390 --> 00:40:26.370
So here, we are assuming
that we know not just

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:30.270
the trajectory in
the state variables,

00:40:30.270 --> 00:40:33.890
but also in the
control variables.

00:40:33.890 --> 00:40:37.110
So in that case, you
have-- so first of all,

00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:39.290
for some of the algorithms
that we have used,

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:42.732
they give you the full
state control pair.

00:40:42.732 --> 00:40:44.690
In other settings-- and
that becomes a bit more

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:48.730
problem-dependent-- if you
are given a state trajectory,

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:53.285
you can backtrack the controls
that give rise to that state

00:40:53.285 --> 00:40:55.410
through the dynamics, for
example, using techniques

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:58.310
from differential flatness.

00:40:58.310 --> 00:41:02.310
So bottom line is either you
use an algorithm that gives you

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:05.430
the state-controlled
pair, or somehow you

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:08.950
have to backtrack
the controls that

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:11.530
give you that state trajectory.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.010
Otherwise, you cannot
really use this formulation.

00:41:16.675 --> 00:41:18.610
OK.

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:20.590
So that's actually quite useful.

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:24.430
And again, note the
very intuitive form

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:27.670
of your tracking-controlled
sequence.

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:31.350
A feedforward term uses
the nominal control

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:35.990
that you would like to
use, plus a term delta

00:41:35.990 --> 00:41:43.830
u that because of LQR is given
by linear feedback on the state.

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:48.750
So it's a closed-loop wrapper
around your nominal trajectory.

00:41:48.750 --> 00:41:49.410
Very natural.

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:51.170
So you have your
nominal trajectory.

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:52.630
Someone pushes you.

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:55.878
And then you have a little
bit of a control algorithm

00:41:55.878 --> 00:41:57.670
brings you back to
where you want it to go.

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:02.630
Let's go one step further.

00:42:06.810 --> 00:42:12.690
Consider now the nonlinear
optimal control problem.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:16.850
And interestingly, let's
now pose the question.

00:42:16.850 --> 00:42:24.593
Can we use LQR to compute an
optimal open-loop trajectory?

00:42:24.593 --> 00:42:26.010
It's a bit weird
because now we're

00:42:26.010 --> 00:42:27.802
talking about closed-loop
control policies.

00:42:27.802 --> 00:42:30.730
So we are using techniques
from closed-loop control

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:34.410
to compute an optimal
open-loop trajectory.

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:37.410
So we'll see why that is
a good idea in a second.

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:39.870
But for now, the
problem is as follows.

00:42:39.870 --> 00:42:43.570
We go back to our general
nonlinear optimal control

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:44.550
formulation.

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:48.490
We want to find an optimal
state control trajectory for it.

00:42:48.490 --> 00:42:51.730
Can we use LQR ideas
to do so to derive

00:42:51.730 --> 00:42:54.470
a new methodology to
compute trajectories

00:42:54.470 --> 00:42:55.650
for nonlinear systems?

00:42:58.790 --> 00:43:01.110
And the idea here is
very similar to what

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:05.150
we did for nonlinear tracking.

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:09.350
But we did a little bit of
twist in the interpretation.

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:12.630
So specifically, let's
assume that now we're

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:15.350
given a feasible
nominal trajectory.

00:43:15.350 --> 00:43:18.050
This could be any trajectory
as long as it is feasible.

00:43:21.430 --> 00:43:25.110
Now the idea is not
that we want to find

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:33.550
a control that brings you closer
to the nominal trajectory,

00:43:33.550 --> 00:43:37.910
but rather a control that
deforms your nominal trajectory

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:39.510
into a better trajectory.

00:43:39.510 --> 00:43:43.030
So we want to get away from
the nominal trajectory, hoping

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:46.790
to find a trajectory
that has a lower cost.

00:43:46.790 --> 00:43:57.990
So you're given a trajectory
here, x bar 0, x bar 1, x bar 2.

00:44:10.250 --> 00:44:14.390
So before-- say that for some
reason you ended up at x1,

00:44:14.390 --> 00:44:18.010
you wanted to compute a control
that was bringing you back

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:23.610
to the nominal trajectory in
terms of the variables delta x.

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:25.730
Now what actually
you want to do is

00:44:25.730 --> 00:44:31.250
to find controls that shift
these waypoints away by quantity

00:44:31.250 --> 00:44:35.490
delta x1 and quantity
delta x2 such

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:42.370
that now you have a new
trajectory with a cost

00:44:42.370 --> 00:44:48.670
c2 that when compared
to cost c1 is lower.

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:54.510
So basically, you want
to perturb each waypoint

00:44:54.510 --> 00:45:00.150
in such a way that you get a
new trajectory with lower cost.

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:04.150
So then the question is,
can we use LQR techniques

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:08.310
to select these kind
of perturbations

00:45:08.310 --> 00:45:09.750
in a principled way?

00:45:09.750 --> 00:45:10.610
Yeah.

00:45:10.610 --> 00:45:11.630
Question.

00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:14.892
So if your-- I guess I'm
confused how you would get us

00:45:14.892 --> 00:45:17.350
into those lessons you want if
you already had your nominal

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:18.430
and assuming that--

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:21.350
But the nominal happens
inside the optimal.

00:45:21.350 --> 00:45:24.310
So the nonlinear trajectory
is a feasible trajectory

00:45:24.310 --> 00:45:26.790
with some cost c1,
but I'm not making

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:28.750
any claim of optimizing for it.

00:45:28.750 --> 00:45:30.030
Oh, perfect.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:32.910
So now I want to get to a
new feasible trajectory,

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:36.710
but hopefully with lower cost.

00:45:36.710 --> 00:45:41.270
So previously, you
mentioned how we

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:43.030
can use the previous
methods to find

00:45:43.030 --> 00:45:45.550
an optimal trajectory and
then the original idea

00:45:45.550 --> 00:45:48.190
for [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:48.190 --> 00:45:53.650
So what situations in
this case [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:53.650 --> 00:45:56.370
feasible nominal
trajectory, but we know

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:58.250
it's not optimal or something?

00:45:58.250 --> 00:46:03.050
So the question is, where
is that nominal trajectory

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:05.450
is coming from for
tracking purposes?

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:07.790
It might be coming
from SCP, for example.

00:46:07.790 --> 00:46:10.770
Here, I'm going to derive
a new method to come up

00:46:10.770 --> 00:46:12.190
with a nominal trajectory.

00:46:12.190 --> 00:46:14.850
And then you will
use LQR to track it.

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:18.010
So that's a little bit the
confusing part that you

00:46:18.010 --> 00:46:19.650
shouldn't be tripped then.

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:20.773
That's right.

00:46:20.773 --> 00:46:22.190
So for now, we are
shifting gears.

00:46:22.190 --> 00:46:23.610
We're not talking
about tracking.

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:26.390
Now we're talking about
trajectory optimization.

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:36.770
So the idea here is
very similar as before.

00:46:36.770 --> 00:46:39.930
We want to reason in terms
of deviation variables.

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:42.890
But now we want to pick
these deviation variables not

00:46:42.890 --> 00:46:48.830
to drive a delta x to 0, but
to drive delta x in such a way

00:46:48.830 --> 00:46:51.230
that we get the new
waypoints that gives rise

00:46:51.230 --> 00:46:54.070
to a trajectory with lower cost.

00:46:54.070 --> 00:46:58.830
So the idea, as before, is
to consider a linearization

00:46:58.830 --> 00:47:00.910
of your dynamics
so that you still

00:47:00.910 --> 00:47:06.150
have linear dynamics
with respect

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:11.190
to your deviation variables
delta x and delta u.

00:47:11.190 --> 00:47:18.270
But now, rather than setting up
an auxiliary LQR problem where

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:22.030
the goal is to drive delta x to
0, you consider different cost.

00:47:22.030 --> 00:47:24.410
The dynamics are the same,
but the cost is different.

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:28.130
Specifically, what
you do is you take--

00:47:31.470 --> 00:47:36.170
you have the cost of
your nominal trajectory

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:39.590
c x bar u bar k.

00:47:39.590 --> 00:47:40.910
This is what--

00:47:40.910 --> 00:47:43.350
OK.

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:48.250
The cumulative cost would
then be equal to c1.

00:47:48.250 --> 00:47:54.330
And then you have the cost of
a general state control per uk.

00:47:54.330 --> 00:48:02.130
So then you can write
c of xk plus delta xk

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:07.140
and c of u [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:11.130 --> 00:48:13.050
in terms of the
deviation variable

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:17.850
only by just using a
Taylor expansion up

00:48:17.850 --> 00:48:20.430
to second-order terms.

00:48:20.430 --> 00:48:27.570
So we are approximating the cost
of a trajectory with respect

00:48:27.570 --> 00:48:29.950
to the nominal trajectory.

00:48:34.490 --> 00:48:36.590
Let me explain this a bit more.

00:48:58.670 --> 00:49:00.870
So the dynamics are
already-- we already

00:49:00.870 --> 00:49:04.110
had the dynamics in terms
of deviation variables

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:08.350
through the linearization
of the dynamics f.

00:49:08.350 --> 00:49:10.550
Now what I want to
do is also to find

00:49:10.550 --> 00:49:14.990
an approximation for the
cost in a way that can

00:49:14.990 --> 00:49:18.010
be used in a LQR-like setting.

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:24.790
Specifically, the cost c
xk uk is equal to c of xk

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:33.650
plus delta xk comma uk plus
delta uk with bars and bars.

00:49:33.650 --> 00:49:34.150
OK.

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:35.070
Oops.

00:49:38.310 --> 00:49:40.230
Simply because remember
that delta x is

00:49:40.230 --> 00:49:44.850
equal to x minus x
bar, and delta u is

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:49.450
equal to uk minus u bar k.

00:49:49.450 --> 00:49:51.170
So this step should be obvious.

00:49:51.170 --> 00:49:54.810
Now I do the approximation
whereby c of x

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:57.610
bar plus delta x
u bar plus delta u

00:49:57.610 --> 00:50:03.570
is equal to c of
x bar k, u bar k

00:50:03.570 --> 00:50:10.730
plus a derivative of c with
respect to x times delta

00:50:10.730 --> 00:50:18.650
x plus derivative of cost with
respect to u times delta u.

00:50:18.650 --> 00:50:21.558
So here, subscript
x means that you

00:50:21.558 --> 00:50:23.850
are taking derivative of the
cost function with respect

00:50:23.850 --> 00:50:25.668
to the x variables.

00:50:25.668 --> 00:50:27.210
Subscript u means
that you are taking

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:29.810
derivative of c with
respect to the u variables.

00:50:29.810 --> 00:50:33.730
Double x means that we're taking
twice derivatives with respect

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:35.970
to the x variables.

00:50:35.970 --> 00:50:39.290
And x u, it means that we
take cross derivatives first

00:50:39.290 --> 00:50:41.430
with respect to x and
then with respect to u.

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:44.530
Here, we're just doing a Taylor
expansion of the function

00:50:44.530 --> 00:50:49.650
c x u around the x bar u
bar up to second order.

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:58.710
So then the idea is let's
compute the deviation variables

00:50:58.710 --> 00:51:05.830
delta u in such a way that
the pair delta u delta

00:51:05.830 --> 00:51:13.430
plus the second-order
terms in such a way

00:51:13.430 --> 00:51:18.230
that the pair delta
x delta u achieve

00:51:18.230 --> 00:51:22.790
an approximated cost that is
lower than the nominal cost.

00:51:22.790 --> 00:51:27.310
So going back to
this drawing here,

00:51:27.310 --> 00:51:30.590
essentially, we are considering
approximation of our cost

00:51:30.590 --> 00:51:35.390
function that allow us to inform
the direction along which we

00:51:35.390 --> 00:51:39.690
want to deform our trajectory.

00:51:39.690 --> 00:51:42.570
Does that make sense?

00:51:42.570 --> 00:51:43.470
Yeah.

00:51:43.470 --> 00:51:47.810
[INAUDIBLE] regularizing
it so that--

00:51:47.810 --> 00:51:50.242
or regulating it so that
the delta becomes 0, right?

00:51:50.242 --> 00:51:52.450
Like the difference between
the [INAUDIBLE] and the--

00:51:52.450 --> 00:51:53.770
Quadratic.

00:51:53.770 --> 00:51:54.490
Quadratic.

00:51:54.490 --> 00:51:55.330
Yes.

00:51:55.330 --> 00:51:57.250
So this one here, it
seems like intentionally

00:51:57.250 --> 00:51:59.050
making the deviation nonzero.

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:02.570
So what's the parameter
that you like [INAUDIBLE] 0?

00:52:02.570 --> 00:52:05.010
[INAUDIBLE] you don't
want to [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:08.490
[INAUDIBLE]

00:52:09.570 --> 00:52:13.270
Well, remember that this is
no longer the standard LQR.

00:52:13.270 --> 00:52:17.690
This is the generalized LQR when
you also have a constant terms

00:52:17.690 --> 00:52:19.970
and you have the cross terms.

00:52:19.970 --> 00:52:25.010
So that's why the delta x here
are not necessarily going to 0.

00:52:25.010 --> 00:52:31.690
If it was only this term
here and this term here,

00:52:31.690 --> 00:52:35.290
then yes, the delta x would
be picked to go to 0 because,

00:52:35.290 --> 00:52:38.750
of course, you are penalizing
them quadratically.

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:40.390
So the minimum will
be when delta x

00:52:40.390 --> 00:52:46.390
is equal to 0, which introduces
also another consideration.

00:52:46.390 --> 00:52:51.110
So before when we were setting
up the auxiliary LQR problem

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:54.870
for tracking purposes,
you as a designer

00:52:54.870 --> 00:52:58.590
picked the penalty
Q and the penalty R.

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:02.950
Here, the penalty Q, the
penalty R, as well as

00:53:02.950 --> 00:53:06.950
the other matrices, are
derived from the approximation.

00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:08.270
So you say, who cares?

00:53:08.270 --> 00:53:13.870
Well, you do care because
you want this problem

00:53:13.870 --> 00:53:15.670
to be well-conditioned.

00:53:15.670 --> 00:53:17.670
For example, you want to
have a Q to be positive

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:22.568
definite, which means that
you may want to regularize

00:53:22.568 --> 00:53:23.610
some of these parameters.

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:27.310
But we'll look at these tricks
and considerations in a second.

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:27.950
Yeah.

00:53:27.950 --> 00:53:32.430
So the idea that using just like
the Taylor expansion to estimate

00:53:32.430 --> 00:53:35.370
with the new costs [INAUDIBLE]
perturb the waypoint

00:53:35.370 --> 00:53:36.770
or reference--

00:53:36.770 --> 00:53:37.890
That's right.

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:38.650
Yeah.

00:53:38.650 --> 00:53:42.350
But you do so in such a way that
the new cost is a quadratic.

00:53:42.350 --> 00:53:45.370
So then you can
use LQR derivation

00:53:45.370 --> 00:53:50.670
to very quickly derive
the solution for delta u.

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:51.370
Yeah.

00:53:51.370 --> 00:53:53.818
So the cross term--

00:53:53.818 --> 00:53:54.610
oh, no, never mind.

00:53:54.610 --> 00:53:57.070
I was going to ask why
there wasn't two deltas.

00:53:57.070 --> 00:53:59.250
There is two deltas
in the cost term.

00:53:59.250 --> 00:54:00.010
That's right.

00:54:00.010 --> 00:54:03.290
So [INAUDIBLE] up here
and then [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:03.290 --> 00:54:06.330
Yeah, I see it.

00:54:06.330 --> 00:54:09.250
So then the idea--
does this make sense?

00:54:09.250 --> 00:54:13.610
So now we have a cost that
rather than encouraging

00:54:13.610 --> 00:54:16.570
the delta x to go to 0 and
encouraging the delta x

00:54:16.570 --> 00:54:21.730
to be chosen in such
a way that you get

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:23.470
to trajectory to a lower cost.

00:54:26.730 --> 00:54:28.330
Delta x equals 0
is not necessarily

00:54:28.330 --> 00:54:29.590
the minimum for this cost.

00:54:35.390 --> 00:54:36.130
Oh, yeah.

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:39.910
OK.

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:43.390
So then, what do you do?

00:54:43.390 --> 00:54:45.790
You linearize your dynamics.

00:54:45.790 --> 00:54:48.430
You quantize your cost.

00:54:48.430 --> 00:54:52.490
You run LQR with respect to the
deviation variables delta u.

00:54:55.550 --> 00:55:00.050
And then you have a bunch of--
you have a sequence of controls,

00:55:00.050 --> 00:55:03.830
so uk plus delta uk.

00:55:03.830 --> 00:55:08.350
What a delta uk's are the ones
that are computed through LQR.

00:55:08.350 --> 00:55:12.470
So now what you do is you
go back into your dynamics

00:55:12.470 --> 00:55:14.270
starting from an
initial condition,

00:55:14.270 --> 00:55:16.670
and you propagate
your dynamics forward

00:55:16.670 --> 00:55:20.190
to obtain the new trajectory.

00:55:20.190 --> 00:55:22.870
That becomes the new
feasible trajectory

00:55:22.870 --> 00:55:26.850
that you use for your subsequent
round of optimization.

00:55:26.850 --> 00:55:30.470
So that's why this algorithm is
referred to as iterative LQR.

00:55:30.470 --> 00:55:33.590
You take a nominal
trajectory in other dynamics,

00:55:33.590 --> 00:55:38.550
quadratize your costs, compute
the optimal deviation variables

00:55:38.550 --> 00:55:43.010
delta u, do that
for all the stages,

00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:45.210
then integrate the
dynamics forward

00:55:45.210 --> 00:55:48.730
to also obtain the sequence
of states corresponding

00:55:48.730 --> 00:55:51.170
to the delta u's that
you have just computed.

00:55:51.170 --> 00:55:55.750
This is the trajectory that
in general has a lower cost,

00:55:55.750 --> 00:55:58.890
and use that as a new
trajectory around which you

00:55:58.890 --> 00:56:00.270
want to repeat this business.

00:56:03.530 --> 00:56:07.010
Now, of course,
there are a bunch

00:56:07.010 --> 00:56:10.370
of approximations that are made
here, so I'll discuss later

00:56:10.370 --> 00:56:13.170
when LQR actually may not work.

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:15.150
But in general,
is the idea clear?

00:56:18.730 --> 00:56:21.670
Similar to tracking, but
just with the opposite twist.

00:56:21.670 --> 00:56:23.850
You want to deform your
trajectory as opposed

00:56:23.850 --> 00:56:25.730
to go to your trajectory.

00:56:25.730 --> 00:56:27.570
Now, of course, to
your point, once you

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:32.070
have computed through iterations
of this algorithm, a feasible

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:34.190
trajectory that
you're happy with,

00:56:34.190 --> 00:56:36.870
then you might set up an
LQR tracking algorithm

00:56:36.870 --> 00:56:39.070
to robustify it.

00:56:39.070 --> 00:56:39.750
Yeah.

00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:44.030
So this reminds me of what we
talked about gradient descent

00:56:44.030 --> 00:56:49.530
[INAUDIBLE] for how close you're
getting with each step of this

00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:50.030
LQR--

00:56:50.030 --> 00:56:52.550
No, not really, because
of the approximations

00:56:52.550 --> 00:56:55.190
that we're making with
respect to the dynamics, which

00:56:55.190 --> 00:56:56.770
brings essentially--

00:56:59.950 --> 00:57:03.030
I'll get back to your
question in two slides.

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:06.910
So here, to iterate,
iterative LQR essentially

00:57:06.910 --> 00:57:09.790
comprises two steps, backward
pass and the forward pass.

00:57:09.790 --> 00:57:12.870
In the backward pass, we compute
a locally linear dynamics,

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:15.590
locally quadratic cost around
the nominal trajectory.

00:57:15.590 --> 00:57:21.030
We solve the Riccati equation
to compute the optimal deviation

00:57:21.030 --> 00:57:23.750
variables delta u.

00:57:23.750 --> 00:57:28.990
And then in the forward pass, we
use the optimal control policy

00:57:28.990 --> 00:57:34.690
uk plus delta u in order to
obtain the state sequence.

00:57:34.690 --> 00:57:37.850
Now, something that
people sometimes get wrong

00:57:37.850 --> 00:57:41.570
is when you do the forward
propagation of your dynamics,

00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:43.990
you don't use your
linear approximations.

00:57:43.990 --> 00:57:46.890
You actually use
your true dynamics

00:57:46.890 --> 00:57:51.108
xk plus 1 equal to f of xk uk.

00:57:51.108 --> 00:57:53.650
Since you know your dynamics
and forward propagating dynamics

00:57:53.650 --> 00:57:57.450
is easy, there is no reason
to use an approximation there.

00:57:57.450 --> 00:58:01.250
So you forward propagate
with the exact dynamics.

00:58:01.250 --> 00:58:02.350
Oh, I have it here.

00:58:02.350 --> 00:58:04.170
Propagate full
nonlinear dynamics

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:06.530
f than the linear
approximate dynamics.

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:08.510
And iterate until convergence.

00:58:08.510 --> 00:58:14.010
Like, for example, as usual, the
cost reduction after a few steps

00:58:14.010 --> 00:58:17.530
is insignificant, or
the difference is in--

00:58:17.530 --> 00:58:20.010
for example, in the
state space, [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:20.010 --> 00:58:21.527
trajectories is insignificant.

00:58:21.527 --> 00:58:22.110
Then you stop.

00:58:22.110 --> 00:58:24.130
Or maybe you have
a maximum amount

00:58:24.130 --> 00:58:28.430
of iterations as a budget.

00:58:28.430 --> 00:58:30.970
So going back to what you know
from last week, of course,

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:33.630
there are a lot of commonalities
with respect to SCP.

00:58:33.630 --> 00:58:34.570
It's very similar.

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:38.510
So you linearize the dynamics.

00:58:38.510 --> 00:58:43.250
And you can complexify your
cost but with a few differences.

00:58:43.250 --> 00:58:49.070
So first of all, in SCP, you
are using a convex solver

00:58:49.070 --> 00:58:51.230
to solve the problem.

00:58:51.230 --> 00:58:56.250
In LQR, you use the LQR
equations that are almost--

00:58:56.250 --> 00:58:59.950
I mean, they are
actually analytical.

00:58:59.950 --> 00:59:03.910
So iLQR is easier to
implement because you're just

00:59:03.910 --> 00:59:06.710
implementing Riccati
equations and then inject

00:59:06.710 --> 00:59:08.110
some derivatives.

00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:10.070
With SCPs, it'd be
a bit more complex

00:59:10.070 --> 00:59:13.350
because you also have to
implement a convex programming

00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:15.350
solver.

00:59:15.350 --> 00:59:17.150
So this seems that--
this seems to imply

00:59:17.150 --> 00:59:19.190
that LQR is better than SCP.

00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:21.310
SCP, though, is a
bit more powerful.

00:59:21.310 --> 00:59:23.830
It's more powerful first,
because it doesn't require

00:59:23.830 --> 00:59:28.650
necessarily a quadratic
formulation of the cost

00:59:28.650 --> 00:59:30.190
and your approximations.

00:59:30.190 --> 00:59:32.590
It could just be a general
convex approximation.

00:59:32.590 --> 00:59:37.330
But most importantly, SCP allows
you to reason about the control

00:59:37.330 --> 00:59:39.410
constraints and
state constraints as

00:59:39.410 --> 00:59:41.820
long as the problem's convex.

00:59:41.820 --> 00:59:45.610
In iLQR and LQR, there
are no constraints.

00:59:45.610 --> 00:59:48.810
If you add constraints to
your problem formulation,

00:59:48.810 --> 00:59:50.570
all the results are
related to the Riccati

00:59:50.570 --> 00:59:54.050
equation don't hold anymore.

00:59:54.050 --> 00:59:57.750
So they are similar in spirit.

00:59:57.750 --> 01:00:00.050
They rely on successive
approximations,

01:00:00.050 --> 01:00:03.710
but they're also a bit different
in terms of complexity.

01:00:03.710 --> 01:00:07.433
SCPs are a bit more complex, but
it's also a bit more general.

01:00:07.433 --> 01:00:08.350
It's also a bit more--

01:00:08.350 --> 01:00:10.950
SCPs are also a bit more
difficult to implement.

01:00:10.950 --> 01:00:12.710
You have to think
about trust regions,

01:00:12.710 --> 01:00:15.690
as we discussed
last week and so on.

01:00:15.690 --> 01:00:22.290
Now, historically, iLQR was one
of the most popular algorithms

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:25.670
because it's super easy to
implement Riccati equations

01:00:25.670 --> 01:00:27.390
plus some linearizations.

01:00:27.390 --> 01:00:29.570
These days, convex
programming solvers,

01:00:29.570 --> 01:00:31.070
embedded convex
programming solvers,

01:00:31.070 --> 01:00:33.470
have become so powerful
that even though you

01:00:33.470 --> 01:00:37.590
have to solve in a line
optimization through iterations

01:00:37.590 --> 01:00:38.410
is not too bad.

01:00:38.410 --> 01:00:42.110
So SCP has also become a fairly
popular direct optimization

01:00:42.110 --> 01:00:42.850
algorithm.

01:00:42.850 --> 01:00:43.390
Yeah.

01:00:43.390 --> 01:00:47.430
So you can really only
use iLQR if you're not

01:00:47.430 --> 01:00:49.470
close to the limits
on your control

01:00:49.470 --> 01:00:51.790
or the limits on your state,
because otherwise, there's

01:00:51.790 --> 01:00:54.850
no reason that your iterations
would go over the boundaries.

01:00:54.850 --> 01:00:55.350
Yep.

01:00:55.350 --> 01:00:56.470
That's right.

01:00:56.470 --> 01:00:57.230
Yeah.

01:00:57.230 --> 01:01:00.270
So [INAUDIBLE] to be
a bit careful in terms

01:01:00.270 --> 01:01:02.890
of how you handle constraints.

01:01:02.890 --> 01:01:07.003
You can handle those
constraints a little bit--

01:01:07.003 --> 01:01:10.210
[INAUDIBLE] for example, you
can add penalties into the cost.

01:01:10.210 --> 01:01:11.750
But you have to be careful.

01:01:11.750 --> 01:01:15.390
But you cannot handle the
constraints explicitly

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:16.670
as constraints.

01:01:16.670 --> 01:01:19.150
Yeah.

01:01:19.150 --> 01:01:22.110
So going back to LQR
to be quick here,

01:01:22.110 --> 01:01:24.370
so there are a number of
considerations that one has

01:01:24.370 --> 01:01:27.290
to consider to make iLQR work.

01:01:27.290 --> 01:01:29.090
First of all, as
also your colleague

01:01:29.090 --> 01:01:32.090
mentioned, you have to make
sure that the new state

01:01:32.090 --> 01:01:34.630
control stay close to
the linearization point.

01:01:34.630 --> 01:01:37.290
Otherwise, all the
approximations that we have made

01:01:37.290 --> 01:01:38.548
may not hold anymore.

01:01:38.548 --> 01:01:40.090
And actually, the
trajectory that you

01:01:40.090 --> 01:01:41.850
compute after one
iteration might even

01:01:41.850 --> 01:01:47.310
have larger cost, which brings
us to the second bullet point.

01:01:47.310 --> 01:01:51.770
So when we roll out the
policy forward in time,

01:01:51.770 --> 01:01:56.570
sometimes you want to
detune the control such

01:01:56.570 --> 01:01:59.090
that you don't get it too
far from the iteration--

01:01:59.090 --> 01:02:02.450
from the trajectory was computed
at a previous iteration.

01:02:02.450 --> 01:02:04.450
So you may do a little
bit of line search

01:02:04.450 --> 01:02:09.050
in order to find the feedforward
term that keeps you close

01:02:09.050 --> 01:02:12.530
to the previous trajectory.

01:02:12.530 --> 01:02:14.390
We need to design a
termination criterion.

01:02:14.390 --> 01:02:16.510
We have already
discussed about this.

01:02:19.650 --> 01:02:22.690
This is an iterative algorithm,
a local optimization algorithm.

01:02:22.690 --> 01:02:26.230
There is no guarantee to
get to an optimal solution.

01:02:26.230 --> 01:02:29.430
So a good initialization
is critical.

01:02:29.430 --> 01:02:31.950
These days, a very powerful
way of initializing

01:02:31.950 --> 01:02:36.230
these iterative
solvers, SCP, iLQR,

01:02:36.230 --> 01:02:38.210
is, for example, to use
data-driven techniques.

01:02:38.210 --> 01:02:39.950
So basically neural
networks that

01:02:39.950 --> 01:02:42.910
have seen gazillions of
optimal control problems.

01:02:42.910 --> 01:02:47.910
You just use the prediction
from the neural network

01:02:47.910 --> 01:02:52.090
as a warm start to
start these iterations.

01:02:52.090 --> 01:02:53.570
So you get the best
of both worlds,

01:02:53.570 --> 01:02:56.830
the generality of
data-driven control,

01:02:56.830 --> 01:03:04.870
but also the assurances
of formal optimal control.

01:03:04.870 --> 01:03:07.310
Cost matrices may not
be positive definite

01:03:07.310 --> 01:03:09.990
when you do your linearization
and quadratization,

01:03:09.990 --> 01:03:12.370
so you may want to
regularize them,

01:03:12.370 --> 01:03:15.550
for example, by adding a
constant plus the identity

01:03:15.550 --> 01:03:18.590
in order to shift the spectrum.

01:03:18.590 --> 01:03:21.650
In general, there is a great
collection of tips and tricks

01:03:21.650 --> 01:03:23.490
in the thesis of Yuval Tassa.

01:03:23.490 --> 01:03:28.690
And I refer you to Section 2.2.3
if you had to implement an iLQR

01:03:28.690 --> 01:03:32.490
and you have a little bit
of issues with tuning some

01:03:32.490 --> 01:03:35.370
of the parameters.

01:03:35.370 --> 01:03:37.950
Now, as a last topic-- and
here will be quite quick--

01:03:37.950 --> 01:03:39.650
I want to talk about
the differential

01:03:39.650 --> 01:03:43.010
dynamic programming,
which is essentially

01:03:43.010 --> 01:03:49.370
a slightly more powerful way of
implementing iLQR-like ideas.

01:03:49.370 --> 01:03:53.530
At a high level what DDP
is doing is the following.

01:03:53.530 --> 01:03:59.050
In iLQR, what we do is to
locally approximate the dynamics

01:03:59.050 --> 01:04:03.570
of the system around
the nominal trajectory

01:04:03.570 --> 01:04:06.830
and locally approximate
the cost, again,

01:04:06.830 --> 01:04:09.090
around the nominal trajectory.

01:04:09.090 --> 01:04:11.730
But once you do
that, then you're

01:04:11.730 --> 01:04:15.970
left with an LQR-like problem
that you solve exactly

01:04:15.970 --> 01:04:18.370
using Riccati equations.

01:04:18.370 --> 01:04:21.030
In differential dynamic
programming, what you do

01:04:21.030 --> 01:04:26.090
is to approximate directly
the Bellman equation.

01:04:29.430 --> 01:04:34.590
Specifically, the idea of
differential dynamic programming

01:04:34.590 --> 01:04:35.570
is as follows.

01:04:41.670 --> 01:04:45.150
So in iLQR, you
approximate your problem,

01:04:45.150 --> 01:04:47.910
and then you run dynamic
programming exactly.

01:04:47.910 --> 01:04:52.310
In DDP, you directly approximate
the dynamic programming

01:04:52.310 --> 01:04:53.950
equations specifically.

01:04:53.950 --> 01:04:56.270
You remember from
last time from--

01:04:56.270 --> 01:04:57.790
yes.

01:04:57.790 --> 01:05:04.230
yes, last time-- that the
optimal cost at stage k given

01:05:04.230 --> 01:05:14.510
state x of k is equal to
the minimum over uk of xk uk

01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:23.330
plus Jk plus 1 of f of xk uk.

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:25.350
So this is the Bellman.

01:05:30.210 --> 01:05:31.550
Is the Bellman equation, right?

01:05:34.370 --> 01:05:43.690
So this is equal to the
minimum over uk plus delta uk.

01:05:43.690 --> 01:05:46.930
Remember that delta u
is u minus u bar, where

01:05:46.930 --> 01:05:50.730
u bar is a nominal
control trajectory,

01:05:50.730 --> 01:06:02.610
c of xk plus delta xk
uk plus delta uk plus Jk

01:06:02.610 --> 01:06:23.750
plus 1 of f of xk plus delta
xk comma uk plus delta uk.

01:06:27.750 --> 01:06:32.270
So let's define the right-hand
side of the Bellman equation

01:06:32.270 --> 01:06:39.270
as a general function
Q of xk plus delta xk.

01:06:39.270 --> 01:06:43.030
Sorry, this should be a
bar, and this should be bar.

01:06:43.030 --> 01:06:45.870
And this should be bar.

01:06:45.870 --> 01:06:50.950
Comma u bar k plus delta uk.

01:06:50.950 --> 01:06:53.910
So this function Q is
referred to as a Q function

01:06:53.910 --> 01:06:56.190
and will play a crucial
role when we talk

01:06:56.190 --> 01:06:57.950
about the data-driven control.

01:06:57.950 --> 01:07:01.750
But for now, we
don't care about it.

01:07:01.750 --> 01:07:04.630
So you see that this
function is just

01:07:04.630 --> 01:07:06.870
a function of x
bar plus delta x.

01:07:06.870 --> 01:07:12.230
And so this entire thing is a
complicated function of x bar

01:07:12.230 --> 01:07:16.130
plus delta x and u
bar plus delta u.

01:07:16.130 --> 01:07:20.130
Remember that in dynamic
programming, Jk plus 1

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:22.450
has already been computed.

01:07:22.450 --> 01:07:24.810
So this is a known quantity.

01:07:24.810 --> 01:07:27.090
f, we know it.

01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:29.230
So we do know this function.

01:07:29.230 --> 01:07:31.490
We know how to take derivatives.

01:07:31.490 --> 01:07:33.930
So the idea of
dynamic programming

01:07:33.930 --> 01:07:39.810
is to locally quadratically
approximate the right-hand side

01:07:39.810 --> 01:07:43.610
of the Bellman equation.

01:07:43.610 --> 01:07:49.167
So minimum of a u bar-- so
this is just a constant, right?

01:07:49.167 --> 01:07:50.250
It's your nominal control.

01:07:50.250 --> 01:07:54.010
Minimum a u bar plus
delta u is equivalent

01:07:54.010 --> 01:07:56.630
of taking just the
minimum over delta u.

01:07:56.630 --> 01:08:00.530
We're optimizing over
the same set of numbers.

01:08:00.530 --> 01:08:05.130
So if you just
then approximate Q

01:08:05.130 --> 01:08:11.530
of x bar [INAUDIBLE] delta x u
bar plus delta u quadratically,

01:08:11.530 --> 01:08:17.350
now you're just solving
quadratic problems with respect

01:08:17.350 --> 01:08:24.470
to the delta u's. X is given.

01:08:24.470 --> 01:08:27.010
x bar is given, so the
only variable is delta u.

01:08:31.790 --> 01:08:34.750
So how do we solve--

01:08:34.750 --> 01:08:38.550
we're just left with a problem
of computing the optimal delta

01:08:38.550 --> 01:08:43.529
u that minimizes the quadratic
approximation of the Q function.

01:08:46.310 --> 01:08:48.430
But it is super easy to do.

01:08:48.430 --> 01:08:53.870
You just take the
derivative equal to 0.

01:08:53.870 --> 01:08:57.270
[INAUDIBLE] do you see
what I'm doing here?

01:08:57.270 --> 01:09:01.729
So in our programming, we
are moving backward in time.

01:09:01.729 --> 01:09:03.689
So this function is known.

01:09:03.689 --> 01:09:05.090
This function is known.

01:09:05.090 --> 01:09:07.270
So everything here is known.

01:09:07.270 --> 01:09:10.430
x is known because we are fixing
it in the dynamic programming

01:09:10.430 --> 01:09:11.270
recursion.

01:09:11.270 --> 01:09:13.250
The only thing that
you're optimizing over

01:09:13.250 --> 01:09:16.378
are really these
delta u variables.

01:09:16.378 --> 01:09:18.170
Now what you do in this
dynamic programming

01:09:18.170 --> 01:09:21.090
is to take this very
complicated function

01:09:21.090 --> 01:09:26.370
and are approximated in Taylor
series up to second order

01:09:26.370 --> 01:09:29.330
by using x bar and u bar
as your reference point

01:09:29.330 --> 01:09:32.370
for your approximations so
that now this optimization

01:09:32.370 --> 01:09:35.689
problem becomes a much easier
problem whereby you want

01:09:35.689 --> 01:09:38.970
to find delta u's that
optimize a quadratic cost,

01:09:38.970 --> 01:09:41.790
which is really the quadratic
approximation, the Q function,

01:09:41.790 --> 01:09:43.450
which is written here.

01:09:43.450 --> 01:09:46.930
This is for the 10th time in
this lecture a second-order

01:09:46.930 --> 01:09:49.450
approximation of a function.

01:09:49.450 --> 01:09:52.170
So how do I minimize this
quadratic function with respect

01:09:52.170 --> 01:09:52.810
to u?

01:09:52.810 --> 01:09:56.270
I take the derivative
and set it equal to 0.

01:09:56.270 --> 01:10:01.330
So the derivative of Q with
respect to delta u is--

01:10:01.330 --> 01:10:03.250
this term is a constant.

01:10:03.250 --> 01:10:04.890
This term is a constant.

01:10:04.890 --> 01:10:06.050
Here, we have a Q uk.

01:10:15.150 --> 01:10:16.690
This term is a constant.

01:10:16.690 --> 01:10:17.890
Here, we have a quadratic.

01:10:17.890 --> 01:10:21.750
So here, we have
a plus Q. Remember

01:10:21.750 --> 01:10:24.430
that the subscript u means that
this is a Jacobian with respect

01:10:24.430 --> 01:10:25.330
to u.

01:10:25.330 --> 01:10:36.218
Q u u comma k of delta u plus--

01:10:36.218 --> 01:10:38.910
so this is a linear term.

01:10:38.910 --> 01:10:40.810
So we take the derivative
also to delta u.

01:10:40.810 --> 01:10:47.790
So we get plus Q x
uk times delta x.

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:51.710
We set all of these equal to 0.

01:10:51.710 --> 01:10:52.870
And then we solve for the--

01:10:52.870 --> 01:10:54.950
which is what we have here.

01:10:54.950 --> 01:10:57.210
And then we solve for
the optimal delta u.

01:11:01.150 --> 01:11:10.490
And that's basically your new
control uk plus delta uk star.

01:11:10.490 --> 01:11:16.910
So you plug this delta
u back into the--

01:11:23.970 --> 01:11:25.810
back into here.

01:11:25.810 --> 01:11:30.730
So what you get is a solution
for Q in quadratic form, which

01:11:30.730 --> 01:11:34.250
means that now your optimal
cost-to-go is also quadratic.

01:11:34.250 --> 01:11:38.290
And then you go
one step backward.

01:11:38.290 --> 01:11:40.570
So basically, what you're
doing in differential

01:11:40.570 --> 01:11:44.250
dynamic programming is to
approximate the right-hand side

01:11:44.250 --> 01:11:49.130
of the Bellman equation by
quadratizing the Q function,

01:11:49.130 --> 01:11:51.290
solving for the optimal
delta u with respect

01:11:51.290 --> 01:11:55.330
to the quadratized
version of the Q function.

01:11:55.330 --> 01:11:57.450
Get the optimal delta u.

01:11:57.450 --> 01:11:59.570
Plug it back into
the cost function

01:11:59.570 --> 01:12:02.970
so that you have actually
a quadratic cost-to-go.

01:12:02.970 --> 01:12:04.790
And then go one step backward.

01:12:08.150 --> 01:12:11.510
So here, I'm writing
down all the derivatives

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:13.070
of the Q function.

01:12:13.070 --> 01:12:15.347
Not too useful to remember.

01:12:15.347 --> 01:12:17.430
The only thing that I want
to highlight here, just

01:12:17.430 --> 01:12:19.910
to draw a comparison
with respect to iLQR,

01:12:19.910 --> 01:12:21.970
is that if you go through
all the derivations,

01:12:21.970 --> 01:12:25.710
you'll see that there are
second-order derivatives

01:12:25.710 --> 01:12:27.310
with respect to the dynamics.

01:12:27.310 --> 01:12:29.550
So essentially, differential
dynamic programming

01:12:29.550 --> 01:12:31.310
is a second-order
algorithm in the sense

01:12:31.310 --> 01:12:33.602
that it also uses second-order
derivatives with respect

01:12:33.602 --> 01:12:34.270
to dynamics.

01:12:34.270 --> 01:12:37.630
While in LQR, you only compute
the first-order derivatives

01:12:37.630 --> 01:12:38.830
of your dynamics.

01:12:38.830 --> 01:12:41.510
So this is an even more
sophisticated algorithm.

01:12:41.510 --> 01:12:44.750
But in terms of--

01:12:44.750 --> 01:12:49.510
intuition is relatively similar.

01:12:49.510 --> 01:12:52.650
So we cover quite a
bit of material here.

01:12:52.650 --> 01:12:54.150
As I said, next
week, we're going

01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:57.590
to cover the topic
of stochastic DP.

01:12:57.590 --> 01:13:00.270
But to recap, so we
have looked at how

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:02.910
to use LQR in a number
of settings that are

01:13:02.910 --> 01:13:04.550
relevant to optimal control.

01:13:04.550 --> 01:13:06.867
Tracking algorithms,
very important.

01:13:06.867 --> 01:13:08.950
Make sure that you familiarize
with that material.

01:13:08.950 --> 01:13:12.210
We also see-- we also
saw how we can derive

01:13:12.210 --> 01:13:14.450
direct methods for
optimal control

01:13:14.450 --> 01:13:21.410
by exploiting LQR results that
led us to the iLQR trajectory

01:13:21.410 --> 01:13:22.730
optimization algorithm.

01:13:22.730 --> 01:13:26.650
And then we saw a different way
of doing trajectory optimization

01:13:26.650 --> 01:13:30.032
that is still inspired
by iLQR ideas,

01:13:30.032 --> 01:13:31.490
but is a bit
different in the sense

01:13:31.490 --> 01:13:34.010
that you directly approximate
the right-hand side

01:13:34.010 --> 01:13:37.170
of the Bellman equation.

01:13:37.170 --> 01:13:39.630
But results are very similar.

01:13:39.630 --> 01:13:42.530
You still get controls
that are a feedforward term

01:13:42.530 --> 01:13:45.575
plus a linear feedback term.

01:13:45.575 --> 01:13:47.950
But, of course, the gains are
going to be different here.

01:13:50.818 --> 01:13:52.610
Bottom line, make sure
that you familiarize

01:13:52.610 --> 01:13:57.570
with iLQR and with
nonlinear tracking with LQR.

01:13:57.570 --> 01:13:58.070
All right.

01:13:58.070 --> 01:14:00.360
So see you on Monday.

